## What it is

LBSM, Latent Behavioral Structure in Low-Dimensional Statistical Manifolds, is an experimental statistical machine learning project. It asks whether adaptive, autonomous, sequential systems show structured, low-dimensional organisation in their behaviour even though what we can observe of them is high-dimensional.

The working hypothesis is that the telemetry such systems produce sits on structured low-dimensional manifolds rather than anywhere in the full space.

## What it studies

- whether behavioural regimes cluster naturally in a latent space;
- whether adaptation, such as reinforcement-guided learning, shapes that geometry;
- whether behavioural drift shows up as the manifold moving;
- whether behaviour over time has a compact statistical description;
- whether any of this carries over from the synthetic setup to real adaptive systems.

Phase 1 uses reinforcement-guided synthetic agents because their regimes are known by construction. They are the first controlled test of the idea, not the limit of its scope.

## The work so far

Seven notebooks cover telemetry generation, manifold learning, HMM inference, online anomaly detection, reinforcement-learning behavioural evolution, manifold visualisation, and a robustness analysis over a 160-configuration grid. The exported datasets and a PDF report for each notebook are in the repository.

## What this is

[caelestia-shell-mango](https://github.com/evoziosk/caelestia-shell-mango) is the Caelestia desktop shell ported from Hyprland to the MangoWC compositor. It is maintained by Evozio, not by me. I use it on my own machines, and this page is about the fix I sent in, which was merged upstream on 19 August 2026.

## The problem

On MangoWC every drawer toggle (launcher, dashboard, session and the rest) did nothing at all. No crash and nothing on screen. The only trace was in Quickshell's log, where every toggle printed the same errors:

```
@modules/Shortcuts.qml[125:-1]: TypeError: Value is null and could not be converted to an object
@services/Hypr.qml[75:-1]: TypeError: Property 'map' of object function values() { [native code] } is not a function
```

## What I found

Two separate bugs:

1. `monitorNames()` in `Hypr.qml` called `monitors.values.map(...)`. On MangoWC `monitors` is a plain array, so `.values` without parentheses is the array method itself, and `.map()` on it throws.
2. The real cause of the silent no-op was in `Mango.qml`. `monitorFor(screen)` built a brand new object on every call, while `focusedMonitor` was a different, cached object. `Visibilities.qml` keeps a `Map` keyed by object identity, so the lookup behind every drawer toggle never matched and came back `undefined`.

## What I changed

The one-line fix for the first bug:

```diff
-        return monitors.values.map(e => e.name);
+        return monitors.map(e => e.name);
```

For the second, `monitorFor()` now returns the same cached `focusedMonitor` object instead of a new one each time:

```diff
     function monitorFor(screen): var {
-        // MangoWC doesn't have per-screen monitor info easily accessible via Wayland protocols
-        return {
-            name: focusedOutput,
-            id: 0,
-            focused: true,
-            lastIpcObject: {
-                specialWorkspace: { name: "" }
-            },
-            activeWorkspace: focusedWorkspace
-        };
+        // single-monitor-only, so return the same cached object as focusedMonitor
+        // rather than a fresh literal each call -- Visibilities.qml keys a Map by object
+        // identity, and a fresh object here would never match on lookup.
+        return root.focusedMonitor;
     }
```

A second commit on 16 August fixed a side effect of that change. A property holding an object literal was creating a binding loop with `focusedWorkspace`, so I turned `focusedMonitor` into a persistent object whose identity stays stable:

```qml
// Persistent object (not a property-var literal) so its identity stays stable across
// reads -- Visibilities.qml keys a Map by this object, and a fresh literal here would
// also form a binding loop with focusedWorkspace, which references this property back.
readonly property QtObject focusedMonitor: QtObject {
    readonly property string name: root.focusedOutput
    readonly property bool focused: true
    readonly property var activeWorkspace: root.focusedWorkspace
    // ...
}
```

## How I checked it

I rebuilt and reinstalled the shell, restarted it, and toggled every drawer repeatedly. The log went from throwing both errors on every toggle to completely clean, the process stayed alive and the drawers opened and closed correctly.

The change touched two files, 19 lines added and 20 removed, in two commits.
